Stockbridge-type vibration dampers for power lines: CBP rulings have classified them under 7326.90 (other articles of iron or steel) or 8546/8547? Insulators are 8546 (electrical insulators), not applicable. Line hardware for power lines is typically 7326.90.8688 (other articles of iron or steel, other), or 7616 if aluminum. Steel is 73% by weight, which is the predominant material, so heading 7326 applies.

I recall CBP ruling NY N301... "Aeolian vibration damper" classified in 8479.89.9499? Machines having individual functions... A damper with moving weights, though, is a passive device. Some rulings classified Stockbridge dampers under 7326.90.8688. I will go with that.

{"hts_code": "7326.90.8688", "reason": "The Stockbridge-type vibration damper is a fabricated article of iron or steel (73% steel by weight, the predominant material) not more specifically provided for, so it falls under the residual 'other articles of iron or steel' subheading of heading 7326."}
